      "text": "We are given the functional equation:\n\\[f(x) + 2f(1 - x) = 3x^2. \\tag{1}\\]\nTo find \\( f(3) \\), we first need to express \\( f(3) \\) in terms of \\( f(x) \\) using the given equation. Let's substitute \\( x = 3 \\) into equation (1):\n\\[f(3) + 2f(1 - 3) = 3(3^2).\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[f(3) + 2f(-2) = 27. \\tag{2}\\]\nNext, we substitute \\( x = -2 \\) into equation (1):\n\\[f(-2) + 2f(1 - (-2)) = 3(-2)^2.\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12. \\tag{3}\\]\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nf(3) + 2f(-2) = 27, \\\\\nf(-2) + 2f(3) = 12.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nWe can solve this system for \\( f(3) \\) and \\( f(-2) \\). Let's denote \\( f(3) = a \\) and \\( f(-2) = b \\). Then we have:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\na + 2b = 27, \\\\\nb + 2a = 12.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nWe can solve these equations by multiplying the second equation by 2 and then subtracting the first equation from it:\n\\[2(b + 2a) - (a + 2b) = 2(12) - 27,\\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\\[4a - a = 24 - 27,\\]\nor:\n\\[3a = -3.\\]\nThus,\n\\[a = -1.\\]\nSo, \\( f(3) = -1 \\). Therefore, the value of \\( f(3) \\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
